# / is the home page itself now — index.html, the riders list — so there is no
# redirect here at all. /riders was its address for a day; keep it working.
/riders               /           301
/home                 /           301

# Pretty per-user URLs -> serve the page (JS reads the name from the path).
# 200 = rewrite (browser keeps the pretty URL). Destinations use the CLEAN paths
# (/profile not /profile.html) because Cloudflare Pages 301-redirects *.html to
# the extensionless URL, which would bounce /user/<name> to /profile.
# The whole shared ranking, which the /rankings summary only shows ten of. Its
# own URL rather than a query string because it is a page you send someone.
/rankings/all         /rankings-all 200

# A park, and a coaster at it. The park leads because a coaster cannot exist
# without one: chop the last segment off a coaster's URL and you land on its
# park, which is a page that exists. A flat /coaster/<name> was never possible —
# 93 names in the database are used at more than one park ("Wacky Worm" at ten
# of them) — while the park+coaster pair is unique across all 1,114.
#
# 200 = rewrite, so the browser keeps the pretty URL, and the destinations are
# the CLEAN paths (/coaster, not /coaster.html) for the same reason the rider
# ones are. The two-segment rule MUST come first: the first matching rule wins,
# and /park/:park would otherwise swallow the coaster segment.
#
# A rename does not need a rule here. Both pages resolve their slug through the
# former-name tables that ride along with /api/coasters, then correct the
# address bar to whatever the current names spell — see findPark/findCoaster in
# app.js. Adding a redirect per rename would be a second, staler source of truth.
/park/:park/:coaster  /coaster  200
/park/:park           /park     200

/user/:name/count     /count      200
/user/:name/rankings  /rankings   200
/user/:name/add       /add        200
/user/:name           /profile    200

# A rider's profile is /user/<name>, not /user/<name>/stats: the page is the
# person, and "stats" was a filename showing through. 301, not a rewrite, so
# every old link, bookmark and search result settles on the short URL — same
# treatment /coasters got. This has to sit AFTER the rewrite above, because the
# first matching rule wins and /user/:name would otherwise never be reached.
/user/:name/stats     /user/:name  301

# /stats held two different pages: a rider's profile and the everyone view. They
# are two files now — profile.html and riders.html — and the bare URL means the
# everyone one, which is what somebody typing /stats was most likely after.
/stats                /riders      301

# The page is /count now, not /rides — it holds a rider's whole count (the day
# log, every ride, and the full credit list), and "rides" was one of the three
# things on it. Same reasoning as the profile losing /stats.
/user/:name/rides     /user/:name/count            301
/rides                /count                       301

# /coasters was folded into the count page as its "Full list" view.
# 301, not 200: the old URLs should settle on the new one.
/user/:name/coasters  /user/:name/count?view=list  301
/coasters             /count?view=list             301
